---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import Table from '../../components/docs/Table.astro';
import { page } from '../../data/docs-nav';
import { CADENCES, DELIVERY_STATES } from '../../data/docs';

const entry = page('/docs/background-sync/');
---

<DocsLayout page={entry} section="Start here">
  <p>
    This is the most common disappointment with every app in this category, and it is almost never
    the app's fault. It is worth understanding once, because the alternative is assuming something
    is broken every time iOS makes an ordinary scheduling decision.
  </p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>

  <p>
    A cadence in Hozz is a <em>request</em>, not a schedule. iOS decides when a background app
    actually runs, based on battery, charging state, network, how recently you opened the app, and
    its own model of when you tend to use your phone. Nothing an app does overrides that.
  </p>

  <p>
    So Hozz does not claim a delivery happened when it did not. It reports the state it is actually
    in, including the boring ones like "there is data to send and I am waiting for iOS".
  </p>

  <h2>The three constraints</h2>

  <h3>Health cannot be read while the phone is locked</h3>

  <p>
    The Health database is encrypted and unavailable until the first unlock after a reboot, and
    protected again in ways that can make reads fail while the device is locked. If iOS wakes Hozz
    on a locked phone, the read fails and Hozz shows:
  </p>

  <blockquote>Health data is locked. Unlock this iPhone and Hozz will continue.</blockquote>

  <p>
    Nothing is lost. The cursor for that type only advances after its records are durably staged, so
    a pass that could not read anything simply did not advance, and the next one picks up the same
    records.
  </p>

  <h3>Most Health types are capped at hourly</h3>

  <p>
    Hozz asks HealthKit for <code>.immediate</code> background delivery on every type it observes.
    HealthKit silently lowers that to hourly for the types that require it — which is most of them.
    Asking for a lower frequency would make the types that <em>can</em> be immediate slower for no
    benefit, so Hozz asks for immediate and accepts what it gets.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is why "When new data arrives" does not mean "within seconds" for a step count.
  </p>

  <h3>Force-quitting the app stops background launches</h3>

  <p>
    If you swipe Hozz out of the app switcher, iOS stops launching it in the background until you
    open it again. This applies to every iOS app and there is no way around it. If automatic sync
    has stopped entirely and nothing else explains it, this is the first thing to check.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="warn" title="Low Power Mode and Background App Refresh">
    <p>
      Low Power Mode suspends background refresh. So does turning off <strong>
        Settings → General → Background App Refresh</strong
      >, globally or for Hozz specifically. Both are outside the app's control and both look
      identical to the app being broken.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>What the cadences actually mean</h2>

  <p>
    A cadence sets the <em>floor</em> — the shortest gap Hozz will leave between two deliveries to
    that destination. It does not set a ceiling, because iOS owns the ceiling.
  </p>

  <Table
    caption="Sync cadences and the minimum interval each enforces"
    columns={['Cadence', 'What it means', 'Floor']}
  >
    {
      CADENCES.map((cadence) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">{cadence.name}</th>
          <td>
            {cadence.id === 'manual'
              ? 'Only when you tap Sync now, or a Shortcut runs.'
              : 'Deliver when iOS next wakes Hozz and this much time has passed.'}
          </td>
          <td>{cadence.floor}</td>
        </tr>
      ))
    }
  </Table>

  <p>
    Behind them, Hozz asks iOS for a background refresh opportunity roughly every fifteen minutes,
    and asks again after every run — a task request is consumed when it fires, so without
    re-requesting, automatic export would happen exactly once. Whether iOS grants any given request
    is up to iOS.
  </p>

  <h2>How to make it run right now</h2>

  <p>
    Open Hozz and tap <strong>Sync now</strong>. Opening the app is itself the most reliable way to
    get a sync: an automatic sync starts whenever the app is opened. There are also two Shortcuts —{' '}
    <strong>Sync Health Data</strong> and <strong>Check Health Sync Status</strong> — so a personal
    automation can trigger one on your terms rather than iOS's.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="note" title="Why “Export now” sometimes waits">
    <p>
      One thing writes an export's spool at a time, because two writers would pick the same next
      part sequence and unlink each other's open file. An automatic sync takes that same writer, and
      one starts when the app opens — so tapping <strong>Export now</strong> a moment later finds it
      busy. Hozz waits for it and names the activity it is waiting for, rather than refusing with a
      message about an export that is not running.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Reading the dashboard</h2>

  <p>
    The dashboard shows one of seven states per destination. Five of them are healthy; only two mean
    something is wrong.
  </p>

  <Table
    caption="Delivery states and what each means"
    columns={['State', 'Healthy', 'Meaning']}
    mono
  >
    {
      DELIVERY_STATES.map((state) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">{state.id}</th>
          <td>{state.healthy ? 'Yes' : 'No'}</td>
          <td>{state.meaning}</td>
        </tr>
      ))
    }
  </Table>

  <p>
    <code>waitingForSystem</code> and <code>waitingForUnlock</code> are the two that get mistaken for
    faults. Both resolve themselves. <code>retrying</code> means Hozz will keep trying on its own;{' '}
    <code>needsAttention</code> means it has stopped and wants you.
  </p>

  <h2>What Hozz guarantees regardless</h2>

  <p>
    Scheduling is best-effort, but correctness is not. Each Health type has its own opaque,
    device-local anchor, and an anchor advances only after the records it covers have been durably
    staged. Each destination keeps its own cursor, so a destination that failed cannot advance past
    data it did not accept, and one broken destination does not hold up a healthy one.
  </p>

  <p>
    The property that matters is this: <strong>an interruption can repeat work, but it cannot skip
      records.</strong> A background window that expires, a crash, or a reboot costs you time, never
    data. Repeats are safe to accept because batch identifiers are derived from the payload bytes
    and sent as an <code>Idempotency-Key</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="/docs/troubleshooting/">Troubleshooting</a> covers what to do when a state is genuinely stuck.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
